Let $1\le n\le d$, and let $\mu$ be $n$-Ahlfors–David regular with $D_E>0$. Then $$\label{eq:global-growth}
 \mu(B(a,r))\le G r^n\qquad(a\in\mathbb R^d,\ r>0),
 \qquad G=9^d C_{\rm AD}.$$ If $D_E<\infty$, then $\mu(\mathbb R^d)\le9^d C_{\rm AD}(D_E/2)^n$. For every $\varepsilon>0$, every $f\in L^2(\mu)$, and every $a\in\mathbb R^d$, the integral defining $R_{\mu,\varepsilon}f(a)$ is absolutely convergent.

Fix $J>1$. For each cube in a finite family $\mathcal A\subset\mathcal D$, choose one test $\varphi_Q$ from the class defining $W_J(Q)$. There is $B_J<\infty$, independent of $\mathcal A$ and the choices, such that $$\label{eq:dyadic-test-bessel}
 \sum_{Q\in\mathcal A}
 \frac{\left|\int\varphi_Q u\,d\mu\right|^2}{\ell(Q)^n}
 \le B_J\int |u|^2\,d\mu\qquad(u\in L^2(\mu)).$$ For vector-valued $u$ the same constant works with $\int\varphi_Q u$ replaced by $\int\varphi_Q\langle e_Q,u\rangle$, where each $|e_Q|\le1$ may be chosen independently.

For every $A_{\rm F}\ge1$ and $\eta>0$ there are an integer $N_{\rm F}\ge1$ and a Carleson family $\mathcal F$ such that every $Q\in\mathcal D\setminus\mathcal F$ has a proper descendant $P\subset Q$ with $$\label{eq:flat-seed-conclusion}
 1\le\operatorname{depth}_Q(P)\le N_{\rm F},
 \qquad b_\mu(z_P,A_{\rm F}\ell(P))<\eta.$$ One may also require $B(z_P,A_{\rm F}\ell(P))\subset B(z_Q,c_0\ell(Q)/4)$. The constants are uniform in $Q$ and the top cube used in the Carleson estimate; they depend only on the fixed data, $A_{\rm F}$ and $\eta$.

Fix $1\le n\le d$, $G\ge0$, and $c>0$. There are constants $A>0$ and $0<\varepsilon_0\le1$, depending only on $n,d,G,c$, with the following property. Suppose that $\sigma$ has global upper $n$-growth $G$, $R>0$, and $\sigma(B(0,R))\ge cR^n$. If $S,W$ are affine $n$-planes and $0<\eta\le\varepsilon_0R$ satisfy $$\label{eq:joint-plane-error}
 \int_{B(0,R)}\bigl(\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,S)^2+\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,W)^2\bigr)\,d\sigma(x)
 \le \frac c2\eta^2R^n,$$ then $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,W)&\le A\eta(1+|x|/R) &&(x\in S),\label{eq:plane-comparison}\\
 |\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,S)-\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,W)|&\le A\eta(3+|x|/R)
       &&(x\in\mathbb R^d).\label{eq:distance-comparison}
\end{aligned}$$ The first conclusion also holds with $S$ and $W$ interchanged.

Fix $0<\gamma<1/2$ and the constants $n,d,G,c$ of Lemma <a href="#lem:plane-comparison" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:plane-comparison">14</a>. There are $\kappa>0$ and $M<\infty$, depending only on these data and $\gamma$, as follows. Suppose $K\ge0$ is an integer, $\delta>0$, $\delta2^{\gamma K}\le\kappa$, $\sigma$ has global upper growth $G$, and, for $0\le k\le K$, $$\sigma(B(0,2^k))\ge c2^{kn},
 \qquad e_\sigma(0,2^k)\le\delta2^{\gamma k}.$$ Then an affine $n$-plane $S$ satisfies $$\label{eq:fixed-plane-moments}
 \int_{B(0,2^k)}\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,S)^2\,d\sigma(x)
 \le M\delta^2 2^{k(n+2+2\gamma)}
 \qquad(0\le k\le K).$$

Fix $1\le n<d$, $c>0$, $0<G<\infty$, $0\le D_0<\infty$, and $0<\gamma<1/2$. Let $\mu_j$ be Radon measures on $\mathbb R^d$ with $0\in E_j=\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu_j$, global upper $n$-growth $G$, and $$\mu_j(B(x,r))\ge cr^n
 \quad(x\in E_j,\ 0<r\le\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}E_j).$$ Assume every hard-truncated Riesz transform on $L^2(\mu_j)$ has norm at most $D_0$. Let $\delta_j>0$, integers $K_j\ge0$, and $A_j,v_j>0$ satisfy $$\label{eq:flat-limit-horizons}
 \begin{gathered}
 \delta_j\longrightarrow0,\quad K_j\longrightarrow\infty,\quad
 \delta_j2^{\gamma K_j}\longrightarrow0,\\
 2^{K_j}\le\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}E_j,\quad A_j\longrightarrow\infty,\quad v_j\longrightarrow0.
 \end{gathered}$$ Suppose $$\label{eq:flat-limit-source-excess}
 e_{\mu_j}(0,2^k)\le\delta_j2^{\gamma k}
 \qquad(0\le k\le K_j)$$ and $$\label{eq:flat-limit-source-oscillation}
 |\mathcal R_{\mu_j}(\varphi)|\le v_j
 \quad\text{if }\ \mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}\varphi\le1,\quad
 \mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\varphi\Subset B(0,A_j),\quad\int\varphi\,d\mu_j=0.$$ There is a subsequence, relabeled by $j$, with the following properties. All the input sequences are restricted along this same subsequence when we relabel them. For each index there are an affine plane $S_j=a_j+L_j(\mathbb R^n)$, tangent and normal linear isometries $$L_j:\mathbb R^n\longrightarrow\mathbb R^d,
 \qquad N_j:\mathbb R^{d-n}\longrightarrow\mathbb R^d,
 \qquad L_jL_j^*+N_jN_j^*=I,$$ such that $a_j$ is the point of $S_j$ nearest $0$, $a_j\to0$, and $L_j\to L_\infty$ in operator norm, where $L_\infty$ is an isometry. Along this same subsequence, $$\label{eq:flat-limit-measure}
 \mu_j\rightharpoonup q(L_\infty)_\#\mathcal L^n,
 \qquad \frac{c}{4^n\omega_n}\le q\le\frac{2^nG}{\omega_n},
 \qquad \omega_n=\mathcal L^n(B(0,1)).$$ Here convergence is against continuous compactly supported tests, and $\mathcal L^n$ in <a href="#eq:flat-limit-measure" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:flat-limit-measure">[eq:flat-limit-measure]</a> denotes Lebesgue measure. The planes obey <a href="#eq:fixed-plane-moments" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:fixed-plane-moments">[eq:fixed-plane-moments]</a> with a single constant $M=M(n,d,c,G,\gamma)$. In particular, writing $e_i$ for the standard basis of $\mathbb R^{d-n}$ and $$\label{eq:flat-limit-normal-coordinate}
 u_{j,i}(x)=\langle N_je_i,x-a_j\rangle,$$ we have $$\label{eq:flat-limit-normal-moments}
 \int_{B(0,2^k)}|u_{j,i}|^2\,d\mu_j
 \le M\delta_j^2 2^{k(n+2+2\gamma)}
 \qquad(0\le k\le K_j, 1\le i\le d-n).$$ Moreover, on every bounded ball, the supports converge bilaterally to $L_\infty(\mathbb R^n)$: each source support point approaches that plane uniformly, and each point of the plane approaches $E_j$ uniformly.

Assume the hypotheses and conclusions of Lemmas <a href="#lem:height-energy" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:height-energy">18</a> and <a href="#lem:moving-compactness" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:moving-compactness">19</a> for a scalar height. We retain their notation and record the conditions used below. Let $\mu_j$ converge on compact tests to $\nu=q\mathcal H^n|_L$, where $q>0$ and $L$ is an $n$-plane through $0$. Here $\mathcal H^n|_L$ has Euclidean normalization, equal to Lebesgue measure in orthonormal coordinates on $L$. Let $u_j$ satisfy <a href="#eq:affine-source-height" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:affine-source-height">[eq:affine-source-height]</a> with $|e_j|\le1$, let $w_j=u_j/\delta_j$, and let $v$ be the common limit given by Lemma <a href="#lem:moving-compactness" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:moving-compactness">19</a>. Suppose $\delta_j>0$, $\delta_j\to0$, $K_j\to\infty$, and, for some fixed $0<\gamma<1$ and $M<\infty$, $$\label{eq:height-source-moment}
 \int_{B(0,2^k)}|u_j|^2\,d\mu_j
       \le M\delta_j^2\,2^{k(n+2+2\gamma)}
       \quad(0\le k\le K_j),\qquad
 \delta_j^{-1}2^{-K_j}\longrightarrow0.$$ Assume that there are radii $A_j\to\infty$ such that, for every $j$ and every compact Lipschitz test $\xi$ supported in $B(0,A_j)$ with $\int\xi\,d\mu_j=0$, $$\label{eq:height-source-oscillation}
 |\mathcal R_{\mu_j}(\xi)|
      \le C(\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}\xi+1)\delta_j^3.$$ The constant here is independent of $j$ and of the test.*



*Then $v$ has finite local fractional energy and satisfies $$\label{eq:limit-height-weight}
 \int_L |v(x)|(1+|x|)^{-n-2}\,d\mathcal H^n(x)<\infty.$$ After identifying $L$ isometrically with $\mathbb R^n$, for every compactly supported smooth complex function $g$ with $\int_{\mathbb R^n}g=0$, $$\label{eq:limit-fractional-equation}
 \int_{\mathbb R^n}v(x)\mathcal Lg(x)\,dx=0,
 \qquad
 \mathcal Lg(x)=\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^n}
       \frac{2g(x)-g(x+h)-g(x-h)}{|h|^{n+1}}\,dh.$$ The integral on the left is absolutely convergent. The same conclusions hold simultaneously for the finite family of normal heights in Lemma <a href="#lem:moving-compactness" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:moving-compactness">19</a>, without further extraction.

Let $n\ge1$, and let $v:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ be measurable with $$\label{eq:rigidity-weighted-tail}
 \int_{\mathbb R^n}|v(x)|(1+|x|)^{-n-2}\,dx<\infty.$$ Suppose that, for every $g\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^n;\mathbb C)$ with $\int g=0$, $$\label{eq:rigidity-compact-equation}
 \int_{\mathbb R^n}v(x)\mathcal Lg(x)\,dx=0.$$ Then there are $b\in\mathbb R$ and a real linear functional $\ell:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ such that $v(x)=b+\ell(x)$ almost everywhere. If $v$ is also essentially bounded, then $\ell=0$.

Let $\mu_j$ be measures, let the frames satisfy <a href="#eq:complete-normal-splitting" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:complete-normal-splitting">[eq:complete-normal-splitting]</a>, and suppose that $\delta_j>0$ tends to zero. Assume $$\sup_j\int\|w_j\|^2\,d\mu_j<\infty$$ and that, for one fixed affine map $v(x)=b+Bx$ from $\mathbb R^d$ to $\mathbb R^{q_0}$, each function $\|w_j-v\|^2$ is integrable and $$\int\|w_j-v\|^2\,d\mu_j\longrightarrow0.$$ Then there are affine $n$-planes $S'_j$ such that $$\label{eq:tilted-plane-distance-limit}
 \int\left(\frac{\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,S'_j)}{\delta_j}\right)^2
       d\mu_j(x)\longrightarrow0.$$ Every integral in this conclusion is finite. No convergence of the normal frames $N_j$ is required.

Fix $0<\gamma<1/2$. Let $\mu_j$ satisfy <a href="#eq:prop-upper" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:prop-upper">[eq:prop-upper]</a>–<a href="#eq:prop-riesz" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:prop-riesz">[eq:prop-riesz]</a> with the same constants, and suppose that $0\in\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu_j$. Let $\delta_j>0$ and $K_j\in\mathbb N$ satisfy $$\label{eq:sequential-horizon-schedule}
 \delta_j\to0,\qquad K_j\to\infty,\qquad
 \delta_j2^{\gamma K_j}\to0,\qquad
 \delta_j^{-1}2^{-K_j}\to0.$$ Assume that $2^{K_j}\le\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}(\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu_j)$ and $$\label{eq:sequential-horizon-excess}
 e_{\mu_j}(0,2^\ell)\le\delta_j2^{\gamma\ell}
       \qquad(0\le\ell\le K_j).$$ Put $A_j=8\,2^{K_j}$. Suppose, for every compactly supported 1-Lipschitz scalar function $\varphi$ with $\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\varphi\subset B(0,A_j)$ and $\int\varphi\,d\mu_j=0$, that $$\label{eq:sequential-cubic-oscillation}
 |\mathcal R_{\mu_j}(\varphi)|\le\delta_j^3.$$ There is one subsequence along which, for every fixed $r>0$, $$\label{eq:sequential-excess-conclusion}
 \frac{e_{\mu_j}(0,r)}{\delta_j}\longrightarrow0,
 \qquad b_{\mu_j}(0,r)\longrightarrow0.$$

Fix $1\le n<d$, $c,G>0$, $D_0\ge0$, $0<\gamma<1/2$, and $\varepsilon>0$. For every integer $J_0\ge0$, there is an integer $J\ge J_0$, depending only on these fixed data, with the following property. Set $$\label{eq:uniform-propagation-constants}
 \delta=2^{-J},\qquad K=2J+3,\qquad A=8\,2^K.$$ Let $\mu$ satisfy <a href="#eq:prop-upper" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:prop-upper">[eq:prop-upper]</a>–<a href="#eq:prop-riesz" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:prop-riesz">[eq:prop-riesz]</a>, let $a\in\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu$, and let $r>0$. Assume $$\begin{aligned}
 2^Kr&\le\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}(\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu),\label{eq:prop-admissible-horizon}\\
 e_\mu(a,2^\ell r)&\le\delta2^{\gamma\ell}
       &&(0\le\ell\le K),\label{eq:prop-horizon-assumption}
\end{aligned}$$ and assume $$\label{eq:prop-oscillation-assumption}
 |\mathcal R_\mu(\varphi)|\le\delta^3r^n$$ for every compactly supported scalar function $\varphi$ satisfying $$\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(\varphi)\le r^{-1},\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\varphi\subset B(a,Ar),\qquad
 \int\varphi\,d\mu=0.$$ Then $$\label{eq:uniform-propagation-conclusion}
 e_\mu(a,r/2)\le\delta,\qquad
 b_\mu(a,r/2)<\varepsilon.$$ Equivalently, the oscillation hypothesis can be stated with $\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(\varphi)\le1$ and threshold $\delta^3r^{n+1}$. The constants $J,\delta,K,A$ are chosen before the measure, center, and radius. The test radius $Ar$ need not be admissible.

Let $d\ge4$ and $2\le n\le d-2$ be integers, and let $\mu$ be an $n$-Ahlfors–David regular Radon measure satisfying <a href="#eq:riesz-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:riesz-bound">[eq:riesz-bound]</a>. Fix $\rho>2$ and a real odd $C^2$ convolution kernel $K$ satisfying <a href="#eq:variation-kernel" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:variation-kernel">[eq:variation-kernel]</a>. For every real $f\in L^2(\mu)$, $$\label{eq:hard-variation-consequence}
 \bigl\|V_\rho((T^{K,\mu}_\varepsilon f)_{\varepsilon>0})\bigr\|_{L^2(\mu)}
 \le C_2\|f\|_{L^2(\mu)}.$$ The principal value $$T^{K,\mu}f(x)=\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}T^{K,\mu}_\varepsilon f(x)$$ exists finitely for $\mu$-almost every $x$, belongs to $L^2(\mu)$, and $$\|T^{K,\mu}_\varepsilon f-T^{K,\mu}f\|_{L^2(\mu)}
 \longrightarrow0\qquad(\varepsilon\downarrow0).$$ The vector Riesz family $R_{\mu,\varepsilon}f$ satisfies the analogous hard-variation bound and has an almost-everywhere principal-value limit, with strong convergence in $L^2(\mu;\mathbb R^d)$. The exceptional null set may depend on $K$ and $f$. These are strong-convergence assertions on each input; no operator-norm convergence is asserted.*

*For the fixed profile <a href="#eq:variation-cutoff" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:variation-cutoff">[eq:variation-cutoff]</a>, every real $f\in L^p(\mu)$ with $1<p<\infty$ satisfies $$\label{eq:smooth-variation-consequence}
 \bigl\|V_\rho((T^{K,\mu}_{\varphi,\varepsilon}f)_{\varepsilon>0})
 \bigr\|_{L^p(\mu)}
 \le C_p\|f\|_{L^p(\mu)}.$$ For every real $f\in L^1(\mu)$ and $\lambda>0$, $$\mu\left\{x:
 V_\rho((T^{K,\mu}_{\varphi,\varepsilon}f)_{\varepsilon>0})(x)
 >\lambda\right\}
 \le \frac{C_1}{\lambda}\|f\|_{L^1(\mu)}.$$ The constants may depend on the dimensions, the fixed $\rho$, the kernel bounds, the uniform-rectifiability data, and, for the smooth estimates, the chosen profile and $p$ when relevant.
